---
layout: default
title: Resources
---

<%= render PageIntro.new(
  section_id: "resources-intro",
  require_path: "headius/resources",
  heading: "Resources",
  body: "Twenty years of JRuby means twenty years of documentation, blog posts, conference talks, and hard-won knowledge. Here's where to find it."
) %>

<%= render Section.new(
  section_id: "blog",
  highlighted: true
) do %>
  <%= render CodeBlock.new(
    language: "ruby",
    framed: false
  ) do %>
# == Headius::Blog
# Recent writing from the people who build JRuby.
  <% end %>
  <%= render Heading.new(
    text: "From the Blog",
    level: 2
  ) %>
  <%= render ArticleList.new(
    articles: [
    {
      title: "JRuby and Leyden: Even Better Startup",
      url: "https://blog.headius.com/2025/09/jruby-jdk25-startup-time-follow-up.html",
      date: "September 2025",
      body: "Follow-up on JDK 25's AOT cache improvements and what they mean for JRuby startup times in production."
    },
    {
      title: "Packaging Ruby Apps with Warbler",
      url: "https://blog.headius.com/2025/10/packaging-ruby-apps-with-warbler-jar-files.html",
      date: "October 2025",
      body: "How to bundle your entire Ruby application into a single executable JAR file using Warbler. Deploy anywhere Java runs."
    },
    {
      title: "Warbled Sidekiq",
      url: "https://blog.headius.com/2025/10/warbled-sidekiq.html",
      date: "October 2025",
      body: "Packaging Sidekiq as an executable JAR — background jobs that deploy like any other Java artifact."
    },
    {
      title: "JRuby 10, Part 1: What's New",
      url: "https://blog.headius.com/2025/04/jruby-10-part-one-whats-new.html",
      date: "April 2025",
      body: "The headline features in JRuby 10 — Ruby 3.4 compatibility, performance improvements, and the road ahead."
    },
    {
      title: "Boosting JRuby Startup with AppCDS",
      url: "https://blog.headius.com/2025/02/boosting-jruby-startup-with-appcds-and-aotcache.html",
      date: "February 2025",
      body: "Practical techniques for reducing JRuby startup time using Application Class Data Sharing and AOT caching."
    },
    {
      title: "JRuby on CRaC: Let's Get CRaCking!",
      url: "https://blog.headius.com/2024/09/jruby-on-crac-part-1-lets-get-cracking.html",
      date: "September 2024",
      body: "Coordinated Restore at Checkpoint — snapshotting a warm JVM to eliminate startup overhead entirely."
    }
    ]
  ) %>
  <%= render MarkdownBlock.new(
    css_class: "resources-more"
  ) do %>
Read more on the [Headius blog](/blog), or browse the full archive at [blog.headius.com](https://blog.headius.com).
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<%= render Section.new(
  section_id: "java-interop",
  highlighted: false
) do %>
  <%= render CodeBlock.new(
    language: "ruby",
    framed: false
  ) do %>
# == Headius::JavaInterop
# The whole point of JRuby, really.
  <% end %>
  <%= render Heading.new(
    text: "Java Integration",
    level: 2
  ) %>
  <%= render MarkdownBlock.new(
  ) do %>
JRuby runs on the JVM, which means full access to the entire Java ecosystem from Ruby. No FFI, no shelling out, no serialisation boundaries — just direct method calls.
  <% end %>
  <%= render LabeledList.new(
    items: [
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[Calling Java from JRuby](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/CallingJavaFromJRuby) — the essential reference for importing and using Java classes"
    },
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[Java Integration Overview](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/JavaIntegration) — type conversion, interfaces, and the integration layer"
    },
    {
      label: "examples",
      body: "[JRuby and Java Code Examples](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/JRubyAndJavaCodeExamples) — side-by-side Java and Ruby for common patterns"
    },
    {
      label: "embed",
      body: "[RedBridge](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/RedBridge) — embedding JRuby inside Java applications"
    },
    {
      label: "blog",
      body: "[Charts with JRuby and JFreeChart](https://blog.headius.com/2025/04/beautiful-charts-with-jruby-and-jfreechart.html) — a practical example of Java library integration"
    }
    ]
  ) %>
<% end %>

<%= render Section.new(
  section_id: "deployment",
  highlighted: true
) do %>
  <%= render CodeBlock.new(
    language: "ruby",
    framed: false
  ) do %>
# == Headius::Deploy
# Ship Ruby apps on Java infrastructure.
  <% end %>
  <%= render Heading.new(
    text: "Deployment & Packaging",
    level: 2
  ) %>
  <%= render MarkdownBlock.new(
  ) do %>
JRuby applications can be packaged as standard JAR or WAR files and deployed to any Java application server. Your ops team doesn't need to learn Ruby infrastructure — it's just another JVM artifact.
  <% end %>
  <%= render LabeledList.new(
    items: [
    {
      label: "tool",
      body: "[Warbler](https://github.com/jruby/warbler) — package Ruby applications as executable JAR or deployable WAR files"
    },
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[JRuby on Rails](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/JRubyOnRails) — running Rails applications on JRuby"
    },
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[ActiveRecord-JDBC](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/ActiveRecord-JDBC) — database connectivity via JDBC drivers"
    },
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[Application Servers](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/Servers) — Puma, Tomcat, and other server options for JRuby"
    }
    ]
  ) %>
<% end %>

<%= render Section.new(
  section_id: "performance",
  highlighted: false
) do %>
  <%= render CodeBlock.new(
    language: "ruby",
    framed: false
  ) do %>
# == Headius::Performance
# Making it fast, then making it faster.
  <% end %>
  <%= render Heading.new(
    text: "Performance & Tuning",
    level: 2
  ) %>
  <%= render MarkdownBlock.new(
  ) do %>
JRuby benefits from decades of JVM optimisation work — JIT compilation, garbage collection tuning, and real threading. These resources cover how to get the most out of it.
  <% end %>
  <%= render LabeledList.new(
    items: [
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[Performance Tuning](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/PerformanceTuning) — JVM flags, memory settings, and JRuby-specific options"
    },
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[Improving Startup Time](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/Improving-startup-time) — techniques for reducing cold-start overhead"
    },
    {
      label: "guide",
      body: "[Concurrency in JRuby](https://github.com/jruby/jruby/wiki/Concurrency-in-jruby) — real threads, no GIL — how to take advantage of it"
    }
    ]
  ) %>
<% end %>

